==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|seriously}}, {{m|enm|sereously}}, {{m|enm|ceryously}}, {{m|enm|seryowslech|t=earnestly}}, equivalent to {{af|en|serious|-ly|id2=adverbial}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪəɹi.əsli/|/ˈsɪəɹ.əsli/|q2=in rapid speech|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪɹi.əsli/|/ˈsiɹi.əsli/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-seriously.ogg|a=US}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# {{lb|en|manner}} In a [[serious]] or [[literal]] manner.
#: {{ux|en|He was hoping that we would take him '''seriously'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Jimmy [[joking|jokingly]] called Bob a [[doofus]]. Bob took the insult '''seriously'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2009-06-26|author=Dwight Garner|title=Out of the Bedroom, Into the Clinic|work=w:The New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2009/06/26/books/26book.html
|passage=Masters and Johnson wanted their work to be taken '''seriously''', and wanted to stay a step ahead of the morality police, so they tended to write in almost comically dense medicalese.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2015|author=Christopher Biggins|title=Biggins - My Story
|passage=Jeremy, meanwhile, was the fun-lover, like me, who didn't always take it too '''seriously'''.}}
# Gravely; deeply; very much.
#: {{ux|en|That was a '''seriously''' unpleasant thing to say.}}
# {{non-gloss|Used to attempt to introduce a serious point in a less serious conversation.}}
#: {{syn|en|no really|no kidding|real talk}}
#: {{ux|en|Now, '''seriously''', why did you forget to feed the cat today?}}
# {{non-gloss|Used to call back to a previous point, in [[disbelief]] or for [[emphasis]].}}
#: {{syn|en|no really|no kidding}}
#: {{ux|en|You baked ten (10) cakes. '''Seriously''', why did you do that?}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} In an [[extreme]] or [[major]] way; [[majorly]].
#: {{usex|en|Unless you're '''seriously''' strapped (armed), you're about to be not okay too.}}

